-- title:
--   Lemma 4.1 — a useful minor of the old quadratic matrix: $\Psi\le\mu(\hat Q)+\mu(H)$
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a finite pseudometric space, $k=m+1\ge2$, $r\in M$, and let columns $q_x\in K^k$ satisfy $q_r=e_1$ (the coordinates after normalization at the request $r$). With $\Psi=\mu(Q)$ the potential (17), $H=[z^{-d(r,x)}q_x]_x$ (18) and $\hat Q$ the bottom block of $Q$ (19),
--
--   $$\Psi\le\mu(\hat Q)+\mu(H).$$
--
--   This is the bound on the old potential in the comparison $\Psi'-\Psi\ge\mu(\hat H)-\mu(H)$ that pays for each request.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: a metric space (M, d) → a pseudometric space (`PseudoMetricSpace M`); reason: the paper, p.1: "our proof works also when d is a pseudometric"; every metric space is a pseudometric space
--   - generalization: K, the fraction field of finite sums ∑ f_a z^a over F = ℚ(γ_{i,x}, ξ_{t,x} : t ∈ [T]), with ν the least exponent → K = HahnSeries ℝ F with F = ℚ(γ_{i,x}, ξ_{t,x} : t ∈ ℕ) (Var, F) and ν = HahnSeries.addVal; reason: the paper's K embeds in the Hahn series field as a subfield on which addVal is the paper's ν (both are multiplicative), so the statement over Hahn series specializes to the paper's K; the paper's F is the subfield of the Lean F generated by the variables with t ∈ [T] (the others never occur)
--   - generalization: the normalized columns of the algorithm before the request → every column family with q_r = e₁; reason: q_r = e₁ is the only property of the state the proof uses; the algorithm's normalized state has it
--   - encoding: k ≥ 2 (standing assumption of §2–5, p.5) → k = m + 1 with 1 ≤ m, first coordinate 0 : Fin (m+1); reason: the same values of k; coordinates 1, …, k become 0, …, m
--   - encoding: µ(G) = min over increasing column choices, for spanning families → minorVal G : WithTop ℝ, the minimum over injections R ↪ ι, ⊤ when all maximal minors vanish; reason: column order only changes the sign of a determinant; on spanning families the two agree
--   - encoding: Q = [z^{−d(x,y)} q_x q_y]_{x≤y} for a fixed total order on M, rows (e_ie_j)_{i≤j} in lexicographic order (and Q̂ its rows with 2 ≤ i ≤ j) → Qmat (and Qhat) with rows indexed by Sym2 (Fin k) (Sym2 (Fin m)) and columns by Sym2 M, diagonal pairs included; reason: the orders only enumerate the pairs; no minor valuation depends on the enumeration
-- source:
--   Coester, Koutsoupias, Zbysiński, The k-server conjecture is true, arXiv:2609.15979 (version of 2026-11-03, sha256 7ca0380a…), Lemma 4.1, p.10 (pdf p.11)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_KServerConj_Psi
import Definitions.Def_KServerConj_Hmat

namespace KServerConj

/-- **Lemma 4.1 (A useful minor of the old quadratic matrix).** Coester, Koutsoupias, Zbysiński,
*The k-server conjecture is true*, arXiv:2609.15979 (version of 2026-11-03, sha256 7ca0380a…),
printed p.10 (PDF p.11): "The old potential before the request satisfies Ψ ≤ µ(Q̂) + µ(H)."

The setting (§4, printed p.10): the columns have been normalized so that the request column is
`q_r = e₁`; `Ψ = µ(Q)` (17), `H = [z^{−d(r,x)} q_x]_x` (18) and `Q̂` is the bottom block of `Q` (19).

**Formalization Note.** `k = m + 1` with `1 ≤ m` (standing assumption `k ≥ 2`, printed p.5), `M`
finite (printed p.1). The statement is for every family of columns `q` with `q_r = e₁`, which
includes the normalized state of the algorithm before every request. -/
theorem lemma_4_1 {M : Type*} [PseudoMetricSpace M] [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] {m : ℕ}
    (hm : 1 ≤ m) (q : M → Fin (m + 1) → K (m + 1) M) (r : M) (hr : q r = Pi.single 0 1) :
    Psi q ≤ minorVal (Qhat q) + minorVal (Hmat q r) := by sorry

end KServerConj
